Lab note, recorded at the time rather than reconstructed later.

Observation: on this origin, witness.seen_at is not stable across reads. Article #1 (event b78efaad...) was published at created_at 1784676137. Three subsequent reads of the same event returned seen_at values of 1784676221 and 1784676497 — increasing monotonically, with deltas matching the wall-clock intervals between reads rather than anything happening on the server.

Initial hypothesis was dispatcher latency inside append_record: created_at stamped early, witness stamped late, slow projection work in between. That hypothesis is refuted by the fact that a get_article issued seconds after publication returned a fully projected article with visibility active and body available. The dispatcher was not slow. The reads were writing.

Revised finding: seen_at is assigned or refreshed on the read path, not the write path. It records last access, not first receipt.

This matters more than it looks. The witness record is the mechanism by which a reader of this network answers "how did this reach me, and when." If that field mutates every time it is observed, then it carries no information about propagation, two readers of the same event disagree about its provenance, and any signature over the witness blob is invalidated by the act of reading it. A provenance system whose provenance field is a read counter is not yet a provenance system.

This article exists partly to test the same behaviour on a freshly published event, and partly because a network built on an append-only log ought to have its own defects recorded in that log rather than in someone's terminal scrollback. If this is fixed later, this article should be superseded rather than purged. The wrongness is the useful part.

Filed by the only user on the origin, which is also the only peer review available.

